{"name":"Lemon Verbena","hex":"#f2ed6b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lemon verbena","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous perennial shrub native to South America, used for its lemon-like fragrance and flavor, Aloysia citrodora."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lemon_verbena"}],"entries":[{"title":"Aloysia citrodora","blocks":[{"text":"Aloysia citrodora, lemon verbena, is a species of flowering plant in the verbena family Verbenaceae, native to South America. Other common names include lemon beebrush. It was brought to Europe by the Spanish and the Portuguese in the 17th century and cultivated for its oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Aloysia_citriodora_002.jpg/330px-Aloysia_citriodora_002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloysia_citrodora","wikidata":"Q206532"},{"title":"Lemon","blocks":[{"text":"The lemon is a species of small evergreen tree in the Citrus genus of the flowering plant family Rutaceae. A true lemon is a hybrid of the citron and the bitter orange. Its origins are uncertain, but some evidence suggests lemons originated during the 1st millennium BC in what is now northeastern India. Some other citrus fruits are called lemon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/P1030323.JPG/330px-P1030323.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon","wikidata":"Q500"},{"title":"Verbena","blocks":[{"text":"Verbena, also known as vervain or verveine, is a genus in the family Verbenaceae. It contains about 150 species of annual and perennial herbaceous or semi-woody flowering plants. The majority of the species are native to the Americas and Asia; however, Verbena officinalis, the common vervain or common verbena, is the type species and native to Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Eisenkraut%2C_Passau.JPG/330px-Eisenkraut%2C_Passau.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Verbena","wikidata":"Q165290"},{"title":"Lemon (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lemon or lemon-color is a vivid yellow color characteristic of the lemon fruit. Shades of \"lemon\" may vary significantly from the fruit's actual color, including fluorescent tones and creamy hues reflective of lemon pies and confections."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg/330px-Lemon_-_whole_and_split.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lemon_(color)","wikidata":"Q2720565"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg/500px-IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg/1280px-IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg/500px-IncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inca Kola soft drink, which is flavored with lemon verbena.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIncaKolaBottleGlass.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Lemon-citrus_limon_seedling.jpg/250px-Lemon-citrus_limon_seedling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Lemon-citrus_limon_seedling.jpg/500px-Lemon-citrus_limon_seedling.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Lemon-citrus_li